  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A warm front tomorrow will bring the next \nchance for widespread rain. \n\nHigh pressure moves offshore with southerly return flow behind \na warm front will bring dew points into the low 70s. Showers and \nthunderstorms will start moving into western areas before \ndaybreak and continue through the day. CAMS producing some \norganization to convective activity largely focused on the I-95 \nurban corridor. \n\nAs for the hazards, there is a SLIGHT (2 out of 4) risk for \nexcessive rainfall. Precipitable water values increase to \nperhaps as high as 150% of normal for this time of year. \nThe hires guidance suggests that heaviest precipitation will be\ngenerally along the I-95 corridor and points northwest. \nGenerally around 1-2 inches of rain with localized QPF values \nin excess of 3 inches. Widespread flash flooding still seems \nunlikely, but the localized threat has increased and have issued\na flood watch for the urban corridor from ILG-northern NJ\n\nFor severe hazards, latest CAMS and HREF guidance has come into\nline with more instability available for storms to work with \ntomorrow. HREF mean SBCAPE values are around 1500-2500 J/kg with\nensemble maximums in excess 3000 J/kg. Deep layer shear \nforecasts have now increased to around 30kt. Boundary layer flow\nis not particularly strong but surface winds are more backed \nthan previous forecasts so an isolated tornado is possible \nprimarily along and west of I-95. Dominant mode should be \nmulticell convection but a supercell can't be ruled out \nespecially where cloud bearing shear is maximized. SPC has \nexpanded the area of a slight risk (2 out of 5) to now include \nmuch of the area. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A cold front will cross the area Friday.\n\nThe timing of this cold front will be critical in the risk for \nthunderstorm hazards. There are differences between models in \nboth the timing, and if this will be a single cold front, or an \ninitial cold front with a reinforcing shot of cold air advection\ncoming in behind it. Saturday and Sunday appear to be dry across\nmost of the area with the exception of southern areas Saturday\nwhere low probabilities of showers and storms will exist.",
